<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Need an Umbrella.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Edit classmates' written work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write short messages about rainy days in a class forum (e.g., 'It is raining heavily in Panama City today.' 'Bring your umbrella and boots.').<br>Can edit classmates' short sentences about rainy day activities (e.g., correct 'It rains' to 'It is raining.').<br>Can revise peers' messages for clarity (e.g., add 'boots' to your message about rain clothes).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0e9f0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a rainy street scene in Panama City (showing puddles, people with umbrellas, raincoats, boots). Students observe the picture. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and guides them to quickly write down 3-4 words they see related to the picture (e.g., <i>rain, umbrella, boots, puddle</i>). This activates prior vocabulary and prepares them for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two sentences on the board about the picture, one correct and one with a common error. Example: "It is raining now. I wear boots." (Correct) vs. "It rain now. I wear my boot." (Incorrect verb, incorrect plural). The teacher says the sentences aloud, emphasizing the correct present continuous "It is raining" and the sound /r/ in 'rain' and /p/ in 'puddle'. Then, the teacher models how to identify the error in the second sentence, circling the incorrect part without correcting it yet, explaining, "This is what we will do today: find mistakes in sentences."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write 'It rain' or 'It is raining' for now?" (Targeting Present Continuous)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we need for spelling words like 'raincoat'?" (Targeting phonemic awareness/alphabet knowledge)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents three short, simple messages (like forum posts) about rainy days written on large flashcards or projected. These messages include examples of present continuous, present simple, and target vocabulary from the 'Rainy Season' theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Message 1: "Hello! It is raining heavily in Panama City today. I need my umbrella. I don't like when it is windy."<br>Example Message 2: "Wow! Look outside! The sky is gloomy. I am wearing my raincoat and boots. I love to splash in puddles."<br>Example Message 3: "It is flooding a little. Where are my boots? I want to puddle-jump!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Narrating current events related to weather: Using the present continuous tense (e.g., 'It is raining. I like puddle-jumping.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads each message aloud. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are these messages about?" (Rainy weather). "What things do people need in the rain?" (Umbrella, raincoat, boots).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher asks, "Which message talks about needing an umbrella?" (Message 1). "Which message talks about splashing?" (Message 2). This ensures understanding of the content before focusing on form.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using Present Simple and Present Continuous. Vocabulary: <i>umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle, wet, rainy, muddy, splash, need, wear</i>. Pragmatic: Talking about rainy weather: 'I need an umbrella.' Sharing experiences: 'I am wearing my boots because it is raining.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion.</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with incomplete sentences about rainy days. Students complete the sentences by choosing from a word bank (e.g., <i>is raining, boots, umbrella, splash, muddy</i>).
                            <br>Examples:
                            <br>a. It ____ heavily today. (is raining)
                            <br>b. I wear my ____ and a raincoat. (boots)
                            <br>c. I like to ____ in puddles. (splash)
                            <br>d. The street is very ____. (muddy)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Sentence Transformation & Writing.</b> The teacher provides prompts, and students write complete sentences using Present Continuous or Present Simple.
                            <br>Examples:
                            <br>a. (rain / now): "It is raining now." (Focus on 'It is raining.')
                            <br>b. (you / need / for rain): "What do you need for rain? I need an umbrella." (Focus on WH-question and present simple)
                            <br>c. (wear / boots / when / rain): "I wear my boots when it rains." (Focus on present simple for routines)
                            <br>d. (puddle-jump / today / I): "I am puddle-jumping today." (Focus on present continuous, sound /p/ in 'puddle')
                            <br>The teacher circulates, checking for correct sentence structure and spelling of key vocabulary (e.g., 'raincoat,' 'muddy').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Writing & Error Spotting.</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes two short sentences about what they wear or do when it is raining (e.g., "It is windy. I need a raincoat."). They then swap papers with their partner. The partner reads the sentences and *circles one word* that might be incorrect (spelling or grammar) without fixing it. This prepares them for the editing task by drawing attention to potential errors.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Product (Peer Editing Activity).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to imagine they are writing a short message (3-4 sentences) for a "Class Rainy Day Forum." The message should describe the current weather and what they or someone they know needs or wears for the rain, including target vocabulary. (e.g., "It is raining heavily. The sky is gloomy. I need my umbrella and boots. My mom wears a raincoat when it is windy.") They should focus on using the present continuous and present simple correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once students finish their draft, they exchange their written message with a different classmate.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student then acts as an "editor." They use a simple checklist provided by the teacher to edit their partner's work. The checklist includes:
                            <br>1. Capital letter at the start of sentences? (Yes/No)
                            <br>2. Period at the end of sentences? (Yes/No)
                            <br>3. Is "it is raining" or "I am wearing" correct? (Yes/No)
                            <br>4. Are words like 'umbrella', 'boots', 'raincoat' spelled correctly? (Spelling focus on 'raincoat,' 'muddy,' 'sky') (Yes/No)
                            <br>5. Does it make sense? Is it clear? (Yes/No, suggest a word to add if needed, e.g., 'add boots')</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use a different colored pencil to mark corrections on their partner's paper. They write suggested corrections above the incorrect words or add missing punctuation. This directly addresses the specific objective: "Edit classmates' written work."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their original message and the edited version from their peer. The teacher collects these to review both the initial writing and the peer editing process.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> The teacher assesses both the writer's ability to produce a coherent message and the editor's ability to identify and suggest corrections based on the checklist.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>For the writer:</b> Use of Present Continuous/Simple (correct verb forms for 'it is raining'), incorporation of target vocabulary (<i>umbrella, boots, raincoat, puddle</i>), basic sentence structure (capitalization, punctuation).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>For the editor:</b> Accuracy in identifying errors (e.g., 'It rains' to 'It is raining'), clarity of suggested revisions (e.g., adding 'boots' for clarity), correct spelling of revised words (phonemic awareness for 'raincoat', 'muddy', 'sky').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write your message about the rain? Why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one mistake you found in your friend's writing?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn today about writing sentences for rainy days?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you still want to practice about writing or editing?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing and editing lesson? (e.g., peer editing checklist, specific examples of errors). The explicit focus on Present Continuous (e.g., 'It is raining') and key vocabulary (e.g., 'umbrella', 'boots') was well-received.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., more examples of WH-questions in writing, clearer instructions for revision suggestions, focusing on one type of error at a time). Some students struggled with differentiating between 'need' (present simple) and 'is needing' (present continuous), requiring more focused practice.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built writing accuracy and peer editing skills. In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their edited messages and share them, potentially reading a revised message from their peer, integrating all skills and preparing for a 21st Century Skills Project related to safety during the rainy season.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>